# The tree is NOT vendored into this repository, and that is a licence requirement

jolt-atlas's LICENSE grants a "personal, non-exclusive, non-sublicenseable,
non-transferable, revocable, limited license ... (i) to download, install, and internally
use the Software solely for the purpose of testing and evaluating it".  Benchmarking it is
squarely inside that grant.  Its §4 then forbids reproducing, distributing, publicly
performing or publicly displaying the Software, creating derivative works of it, and
reverse engineering it.

So, exactly as with `systems/deepprove/`, **no part of jolt-atlas's source is present
anywhere in this repository.**  The tree was cloned OUTSIDE the repository; this directory
holds the pinned commit above, the exact commands, and **our own** measurements.

## Its licence is the same instrument as DeepProve's, but it is not the same file

Worth stating precisely, because "the same licence" is the sort of claim that gets repeated
without being checked.  We diffed the two files.

  same    §1 (defined terms), §2 (the grant, including the testing-and-evaluation clause
          quoted above), §3, §4 (the restrictions), §5-§8 — word for word once the party
          name is normalised.
  differ  the party and the year.  jolt-atlas: "Copyright 2025 ICME."  DeepProve:
          "Copyright 2024 Lagrange Labs, Inc."  DeepProve's file also spells the party
          "LAGRANGE" in some of the all-caps passages where jolt-atlas spells it "ICME".

So: **the same licence instrument, filled in by two different companies** — not a copy of
one file.  The operative sections that decide what this benchmark may do are identical.

## What that costs, and what it does not

1. **No internal instrumentation, and no patching.**  Not one line of jolt-atlas was
   modified.  Every figure in `RESULTS.md` is produced either by `/usr/bin/time -l` around
   the process or by jolt-atlas's own public API called from our harness.
2. **The harness is ours and lives outside their tree.**  `bench/scripts/jolt-atlas/harness/`
   is a separate Cargo workspace that depends on the clone by path and calls only public
   items.  It contains no jolt-atlas code.  This is the same arrangement `systems/binius64/`
   used, and it is available here because jolt-atlas exposes a usable public API —
   `Model::load`, `AtlasSharedPreprocessing::preprocess`, `ONNXProof::prove`,
   `ONNXProof::verify`, `CanonicalSerialize` on the proof — which DeepProve's ONNX path did
   not.
3. **No format reverse engineering.**  The correctness control corrupts the serialized proof
   at byte offsets and asks jolt-atlas's own verifier to judge it.  Which byte belongs to
   which field is deliberately NOT established; §4(b) forbids it.  Offsets are reported as
   offsets.
4. **One asymmetry against DeepProve, declared.**  DeepProve could not be measured through a
   library API at all, so its `prove` bracket had to include quantized inference and its
   proof size had to include the verifier context.  jolt-atlas's brackets are tighter.
   **That is a difference in what we could measure, not a difference in the systems**, and
   no comparison in `RESULTS.md` is made without saying so in the same sentence.

# Local modifications to the pinned tree

**None.**  `git status` is clean at `434ab99`.

One directory was ADDED to the clone by jolt-atlas's own script and is not a modification of
their source: `atlas-onnx-tracer/models/gpt2/`, written by `scripts/download_gpt2.py`
(their file, run unmodified) to materialise the GPT-2 ONNX export their README's
"Getting Started" section instructs the user to download.  It is not committed here.

A second working tree of the SAME repository exists in the scratch directory at commit
`53b7c873a6662cdc79d9818dececf337bb27d7d0` (2026-05-06), created with `git worktree add`.
It exists for one purpose, `REPRODUCTION.md` §3: that is the commit whose README publishes
the benchmark figures, and reproducing a number at the commit that published it is the only
way to tell a machine difference from a tree difference.  It is also pristine.

